Copy Constructor:
-----------------
	A copy constructor is a special constructor that initializes a 
	new object by copying the data of an existing object of 
	the same class.


	Syntax: A copy constructor takes a reference to an object of the same class:
		ClassName(const ClassName& source);
	
		. const means the source object will not be modified.
		. & means the source object is passed by reference.
	
	Why pass by reference?
		If we wrote:
			Player(Player source);
		
		The compiler would need to create a copy of source to pass it by value. 
		That copy would require calling the copy constructor again, resulting 
		in recursive copying.


	Key Points:
		1. It creates a new object from an existing object.
		
		ex:
			GameCharacter warrior{"Warrior", 100, 50};
			GameCharacter copy{warrior};

		Here, copy is created using the data from warrior.


		2. It takes a reference to an object of the same class.

		ex:
			GameCharacter(const GameCharacter& source);

		3. It is normally declared with 'const' and reference (&).
		
		ex:
			const GameCharacter& source

		4. The copy constructor is called automatically in several situations, such as:
			a. Direct initialization:
				Player player2{player1};

			b. Copy initialization:
				Player player2 = player1;

			c. Passing an object by value:
				Player Raj {"Raj", 90, 10};
				
				void display(Player x)
				{
    					// x is copy of Raj
					// x is local to display
				}

				display(Raj);

			The parameter player is a copy of player1.

			d . Returning an object by value:
				Player p;
				
				Player create_player()
				{
				    Player temp{"Hero", 100, 50};
				    return temp; // copy of temp is returned
				}
				p = create_player();
				
			However, copy elision/RVO may allow the compiler to avoid the actual copy.

			e. Compiler-Generated Copy Constructor:
				If you don't define a copy constructor, C++ can automatically 
				generate one for the class. 

			ex:
				Player player1{"Hero", 100, 50};
				Player player2{player1};
	

		5. A copy constructor can be explicitly defined by the programmer.
		
		ex:
			GameCharacter::GameCharacter(const GameCharacter& source)
    				: character_name{source.character_name},
      				  health{source.health},
      				  experience{source.experience}
			{
			}

		6. If you don't write one, C++ normally provides an implicit copy constructor that 
		   performs member-wise copying.


		7. Copy constructor vs normal constructor:

		ex:
			
			GameCharacter("Warrior", 100, 50);  // Normal constructor

		creates an object from individual values.

		ex:
			GameCharacter copy{warrior};        // Copy constructor

		creates an object from another GameCharacter object


		

	
